William Glasgow-1087 fits the broad chronology: he would have been about 34 at the first event and about 45 at the last. The 1648 event also occurred in the parish where he had been christened. These facts make him a reasonable candidate, but no entry identifies the adult father as the William christened in 1609.

The National Records of Scotland describes Kirknewton and East Calder as formerly separate parishes which were not united until 1751.<ref name="NRSKirknewton">National Records of Scotland, '''CH2/412''', Records of Kirknewton / Kirknewton and East Calder Kirk Session, 1646–1963; [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/browseDetails.aspx?reference=CH2%2F412 NRS catalogue].</ref> The combined FamilySearch place label therefore does not establish which seventeenth-century parish contains the 1643, 1649, 1652 or 1655 entries.

Walter's September 1649 event falls within the documented gap in the Mid Calder baptism register. It must not be used as evidence of a Mid Calder baptism without the original image.

=== Profile and relationship conflicts ===

* [[Glasgow-3515|William Glasgow]] is a profile reconstructed around an adult William named in later child records. The record for Margreat in 1643 identifies her father as William but does not identify that adult William's own parents. Glasgow-3515 is not a documented son of William Glasgow-1087.
* The former adult-father profile created around the 1648 Mid Calder baptisms was merged into this William on 27 September 2026. It had earlier absorbed the Kirknewton/East Calder child cluster. The age and Mid Calder locality support identifying the reconstructed adult with the 1609 child, but this is not a direct cross-record identification. The former placement of the adult as son of [[Glasgow-1084|John Glasgow]], christened in 1616, was unsupported and chronologically implausible.
* [[Glasgow-3610|John Glasgow]] is documented only as the father of a son Johne christened on 22 April 1649. That record does not identify the adult John's parents.<ref name="FS1649John">"Scotland, Births and Baptisms, 1564–1950," database, FamilySearch, entry for Johne Glasgow, christened 22 April 1649 at Kirknewton and East Calder, father Johne Glasgow; [https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:VQ4K-X6G FamilySearch record VQ4K-X6G].</ref> He is not a documented son of William Glasgow-1087.
* [[Glasgow-2454|Issobel Glasgow]] and [[Glasgow-3654|Isobel Glasgow]] appear to represent the same 3 February 1648 baptism and should be compared as a probable duplicate pair.
* The merger keeps the documented 1648 children with their named adult father and treats his identification with the 1609 baptism as inferred. The 1643, 1649, 1652 and 1655 child events should still be tested individually. [[Glasgow-1319|Andrew Glasgow]] remains only a possible relative, although an uncertain tree link is retained.

=== Same-name William in Leith ===

A published Edinburgh testament index records '''William Glasgow, mealmaker in Leith''', with a testament registered on 22 July 1646.<ref name="LeithTestamentIndex">Francis J. Grant, ed., ''The Commissariot Record of Edinburgh: Register of Testaments, Part II, Volumes 35 to 81, 1601–1700'' (Edinburgh: Scottish Record Society, 1898), p. 157, entry for William Glasgow, mealmaker in Leith, 22 July 1646; [https://archive.org/details/scottishrecordso02scotuoft/page/157/mode/2up Internet Archive]. The indexed date is the testament-register date and is not necessarily the date of death.</ref>

The Edinburgh apprentice register separately records Archibald Glasgow, son of William Glasgow, mealmaker in Leith, booked as apprentice to Thomas Park, cordiner, on 23 October 1639. The editor states that the dates in the volume are the dates on which the indentures were booked.<ref name="LeithApprentice">Francis J. Grant, ed., ''The Register of Apprentices of the City of Edinburgh, 1583–1666'' (Edinburgh: Scottish Record Society, 1906), p. 71, entry for Archibald Glasgow; [https://archive.org/details/scottishrecordso28scotuoft/page/71/mode/2up Internet Archive].</ref>

William Glasgow-1087 was only about 29 at the apprentice-register date. A son already appearing in an apprenticeship record makes the Leith mealmaker more likely to have been a different, older William. This is probable rather than proved. The testament contents are required before assigning or excluding it conclusively.

=== Priority Research ===

* Obtain and transcribe the original 24 December 1609 Mid Calder entry in '''OPR 694/10'''.
** Record the complete wording, manuscript surname spelling, whether a separate birth date appears, the father's residence or occupation, witnesses and adjoining entries.
** Do not expect the entry to name William's mother.

* Obtain the original 1608 entry behind FamilySearch record '''X1BL-X8B'''.
** Compare the father's wording, residence, occupation, witnesses and page context directly with the 1609 entry.
** Treat [[Glasgow-1086|Jhone Glasgow]] only as a possible brother until that comparison is made.

* Obtain the original 3 February 1648 entry, '''Mid Calder OPR 694/10/94'''.
** Establish the precise wording for Johne, Issobel and their father William.
** Record residence, occupation, witnesses and whether the two baptisms are separate entries.
** Compare these details with the 1609 entry to test the current inferred identification of the adult father.

* Obtain the original entries behind FamilySearch records '''VQ4K-8Q5''', '''VQ4K-FWL''', '''VQ4K-H58''' and '''VQ4K-H5K'''.
** Determine whether each entry belongs to Kirknewton or East Calder.
** Compare residences, occupations, witnesses and manuscript spelling with the 1648 Mid Calder entry.
** Look deliberately for evidence that more than one adult William was registering children.

* Obtain the testament of '''William Glasgow, mealmaker in Leith''', indexed 22 July 1646, from National Records of Scotland series '''CC8/8'''.<ref name="NRSTestaments">National Records of Scotland, '''CC8/8''', Register of Testaments, 1514–1850; [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/browseDetails.aspx?reference=CC8%2F8 NRS catalogue].</ref>
** Extract any death wording, widow, children, executors, cautioners, residence, debtors, creditors and inventory.
** Compare all named family members with Archibald Glasgow's 1639 apprenticeship.

* Search the Midcalder kirk-session records, beginning with '''CH2/266/1''', and the Kirknewton records, beginning with '''CH2/412/1'''.<ref name="NRSMidcalder">National Records of Scotland, '''CH2/266''', Records of Midcalder / Midcalder St John's / Kirk of Calder Kirk Session, 1604–1977; [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/browseDetails.aspx?reference=CH2%2F266 NRS catalogue].</ref>
** Search Glasgow, Glassgow, Glasgo, Glascow and clerk variants.
** Prioritise marriage proclamations, residence, occupation, cautioners, discipline involving pregnancy, poor relief, certificates and repeated associates.
** Respect the documented 1649–1651 Mid Calder gap.

